{
  "timestamp": "2026-08-10T13:41:09Z",
  "title": "NS -- Hero Video On The Donate Page",
  "type": "feature",
  "userRequest": "Queued plan ns--hero-video-on-the-donate-page: let the Momentum Fund hero on /donate carry a video instead of the photo, set from a new Hero video field in /admin.",
  "description": "The Momentum Fund hero can now carry a video. A new Hero video field on the /donate page-copy entry takes a path like /videos/momentum.mp4; when set, the hero plays it silently on a loop behind the headline, subhead and Give button. Blank leaves the page exactly as it is today, which is production until real footage exists.\n\nThe fallback is the design's centre of gravity, and it is structural rather than defensive. heroImage does not step aside when a video is set: it stays as the section's CSS background AND becomes the video's poster. So a path that 404s, a browser that refuses to autoplay, and a visitor on prefers-reduced-motion all land on the current page rather than a black rectangle — and none of those three paths involves any JavaScript. The video sits beneath the existing scrim, which already guaranteed the headline's contrast floor over an arbitrary photo and now does the same over an arbitrary frame of video.\n\nTwo decisions are worth restating because they look wrong until you know why. The field is `text`, not the `image` type used beside it: the media library uploader is images-only, so an image-typed field would render a picker that could never list a video — a control that looks right and cannot work. And heroVideo is deliberately absent from donatePage.json: every other frame field falls back to that JSON when the CMS box is blank, which applied here would make the video impossible to REMOVE from /admin, since clearing the box would resurrect the JSON value.\n\nBeyond the plan, at the user's request, docs/editing-the-site.md now documents the field for the site's editor — including the two things she would otherwise learn the hard way: reduced-motion visitors are shown the photo by design, and a wrong path fails SILENTLY, so the way to check a video is really working is to look at the page rather than trust the saved field. A bullet claiming the donate hero was not editable was corrected in the same pass; it would have sent her away from the field she needs.\n\npublic/videos/sample-backdrop.mp4 is a 10 KB placeholder, not campaign footage — a flat gradient whose frames are all identical, so scenario captures are stable rather than depending on when the screenshot fired. Its README says so and says what real footage should be.\n\n6 tests: the merge passthrough, blank-clears-to-undefined (the reason the key is absent from the JSON), the frame-keys guard, and the registry field's shape — that it is optional `text` with a hint, and sits directly after Hero image. The shape assertions stand in for a screenshot: the field renders below a viewport-sized capture and the harness cannot scroll, so a durable check replaces a screenshot that would have had to frame the right scroll position. 720 pass in total.\n\nTwo things no capture can show, stated so the scenario set is not mistaken for more than it is. No screenshot shows the video PLAYING: the headless capture browser never starts playback, so the video element shows its poster — which is the hero image — and a VP9 WebM behaves identically, ruling out a codec gap. And the /admin field itself is below the fold of a viewport capture. Both are named in the scenario descriptions rather than implied away.\n\nThe Demo gate is a recorded structural skip for the same container harness fault as the previous two features: the editor binds non-loopback, so its control API requires a token the capture browser cannot attach, and every preview-interact / preview-flow / raw-path preview returns 401.",
